<circuit version="1.1.0-SR2" rev="261110" stepSize="1000000" stepsPS="1000000" NLsteps="100000" reaStep="10000" animate="0" >
<description>
# Series RLC bandpass: numerical damping and step convergence

The source, 120 nF capacitor, 50 mH inductance and 54 ohm resistor form a series
loop. The transformer variant uses exactly one primary segment (Primary=1,
Secondary=0), not an open-ended, two-segment winding. Probe GEN.outnod and R1.lPin.

The continuous-time resonance is 2054.681 Hz. At 2055 Hz the ideal resistor/input
RMS ratio is 0.999993. The file uses a 10 ns reactive step: expect approximately
0.99845 with the inductor, or 0.99841 with the transformer (its winding has an
additional 2 milliohm resistance). These are [SIMULATED] model expectations.

Backward Euler deliberately introduces numerical damping. With the simulation
stopped, change Reactive Step to 100 ns or 1 us, restart, and expect gains near
0.98479 or 0.86629 respectively. This is not physical winding resistance or a
double-counted current. Around resonance the effective numerical resistance of
the L/C pair is h/C. Lowering Simulation Step Size alone does not lower h.

For CLI measurements use one sim average call for input and output over an integer
number of source periods. The regression tests also cover DC steps, restarting
an idle inductor, free-decay energy, and convergence towards continuous physics.
</description>


